<%#
  The items inside a menu panel, shared by `row_actions` and `menu_button` so that what a menu item
  *is* -- its roles, its tones, and the difference between a disabled link and a disabled form
  control -- has one definition rather than two that drift.

  Locals:
    items  required; each {label:, path:, icon:, method:, confirm:, tone:, disabled:, reason:,
           link_options:}
%>
<% items.compact.each do |item| %>
  <%
    tone_classes = (item[:tone] == :danger) ?
      "text-slate-700 hover:bg-rose-50 hover:text-rose-700" :
      "text-slate-700 hover:bg-slate-50 hover:text-slate-900"
    item_classes = "flex w-full items-center gap-2 px-3 py-2 text-left text-sm #{tone_classes} " \
                   "focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-brand-600"
  %>
  <% if item[:disabled] %>
    <%#
      An unavailable *form* action is a genuinely `disabled` <button>, and an unavailable *link*
      action is a non-interactive <span> with `aria-disabled` -- design.md draws that line and it
      matters: `disabled` is only a thing a form control can be.

      **The reason is visible.** It used to be `sr-only`, which meant a screen reader heard
      "Deactivate, unavailable while this item is still in inventory or used by a kit" and everyone
      else saw a greyed-out word and nothing at all -- reported as confusing, and rightly. It is a
      second line under the label now: read by everyone, no hover, no click. Polaris calls this
      `helpText` on an action list item.

      Not a tooltip: a `disabled` control fires no pointer events, so a tooltip on one needs a
      wrapper element to work at all, and there is no hover on a phone.

      `items-start` because the item is two lines now and the glyph belongs beside the first.
    %>
    <%#
      The dimming is on the *label*, not on the whole item, so the reason is not dimmed with it.
      `opacity-60` on the container painted the help text at **2.32:1** measured against white --
      WCAG 1.4.3 exempts an inactive control from contrast, but the entire point of showing the
      reason is that it gets read, and a rule that lets you dim it does not make dimming it right.
      At full opacity slate-500 is 4.8:1.
    %>
    <% body = capture do %>
      <i class="<%= item[:icon] %> mt-0.5 opacity-60" aria-hidden="true"></i>
      <span class="min-w-0">
        <span class="opacity-60"><%= item[:label] %></span>
        <% if item[:reason] %>
          <span class="mt-0.5 block text-xs font-normal text-slate-500"><%= item[:reason] %></span>
        <% end %>
      </span>
    <% end %>
    <% disabled_classes = "#{item_classes} items-start" %>
    <% if item[:method] %>
      <%# `form: {role: "none"}` on the wrapper: `role="menu"` must own its `menuitem`s, and a
          <form> in between is a third thing in the tree that is neither. `none` makes it
          transparent, the same trick an <li> wrapper needs in a menu. %>
      <%= button_to item[:path], method: item[:method], disabled: true,
            form_class: "block", form: {role: "none"}, role: "menuitem",
            class: disabled_classes do %>
        <%= body %>
      <% end %>
    <% else %>
      <span class="<%= disabled_classes %>" role="menuitem" aria-disabled="true"><%= body %></span>
    <% end %>
  <% elsif item[:method] %>
    <%#
      A plain `button_to` rather than `essentials_action_button`, because that helper is for a
      button and these are menu items.

      It applied `essentials_button_classes` -- `inline-flex justify-center` plus the size's own
      padding -- and wrapped the form in `form_class: "inline-block"`. Inside a menu that is bad
      twice: `inline-flex` beat the item's `flex w-full`, and an inline-block form in a cell that
      is `text-right` shrink-wraps and floats to the right edge. Measured on /vendors: *Edit* sat
      at x=1 across the full 222px, and *Deactivate* at **x=117, 106px wide** -- half width, hard
      right, in the same menu. Every enabled action in every row menu looked like that.

      `data-turbo=false` is kept and matters: these sit inside a results turbo-frame, and Turbo
      intercepting the submit is what made Reactivate silently do nothing about half the time.
      See `essentials_action_button` for the full account.
    %>
    <%= button_to item[:path], method: item[:method],
          form_class: "block", form: {role: "none"}, role: "menuitem", class: item_classes,
          data: {turbo: false, disable_with: "Please wait...", confirm: item[:confirm]}.compact do %>
      <i class="<%= item[:icon] %>" aria-hidden="true"></i><%= item[:label] %>
    <% end %>
  <% else %>
    <%= link_to item[:path], class: item_classes, role: "menuitem", **(item[:link_options] || {}) do %>
      <i class="<%= item[:icon] %>" aria-hidden="true"></i><%= item[:label] %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
